\fs96 \cf2 Types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 All values in Mojo have an associated data type. Most of the types are nominal types, defined by a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/structs/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 struct}}. These types are nominal (or "named") because type equality is determined by the type's name, not its structure.\
There are some types that aren't defined as structs:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Functions are typed based on their signatures.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
NoneType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a type with one instance, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 None
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  object, which is used to signal "no value."\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo comes with a standard library that provides a number of useful types and utility functions. These standard types aren't privileged. Each of the standard library types is defined just like user-defined types\'97even basic types like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/int/Int/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Int}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/string/string/String/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 String}}. But these standard library types are the building blocks you'll use for most Mojo programs.\
The most common types are built-in types, which are always available and don't need to be imported. These include types for numeric values, strings, boolean values, and others.\
The standard library also includes many more types that you can import as needed, including collection types, utilities for interacting with the filesystem and getting system information, and so on.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Numeric types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo provides built-in numeric types that represent signed integers, unsigned integers, and floating-point values. These types support multiple precisions and are used to model both low-level data and high-level numeric computation.\
The following sections introduce integer and floating-point types in Mojo.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 All numeric types support the usual numeric and bitwise operators. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/math/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb14 \ul math}} module provides additional math functions.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Integers and unsigned integers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo supports both signed (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) and unsigned (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) integer types. These include both fixed-size and general types:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If you need a fixed-size integer, Mojo provides explicit-width integer types such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int8
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int16
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UInt32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UInt64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . These fixed-precision integer types are aliases to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/simd/SIMD/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 SIMD}} type.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Use the general 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  types when you don't require a specific bit width.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  represents a signed integer that uses the system's native word size, typically 64 bits on 64-bit CPUs and 32 bits on 32-bit CPUs. Similarly, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  represents an unsigned integer that uses the system's default word size.\
You may wonder when to use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and when to use the other integer types. In general, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a good safe default when you need an integer type and you don't require a specific bit width. Using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as the default integer type for APIs makes APIs more consistent and predictable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Signed versus unsigned\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Signed and unsigned integers with the same bit width can represent the same number of distinct values, but over different ranges. For example:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Int8
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  represents 256 values ranging from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -128
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 127
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
\ls6\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
UInt8
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  represents 256 values ranging from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 0
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 255
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Overflow behavior\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Signed and unsigned integers differ in how they handle overflow.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When a signed integer overflows, the value wraps around into the negative range using two's complement arithmetic. For example, adding 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 1
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var si: Int8 = 127
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  results in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -128
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When an unsigned integer overflows, the value wraps around to the beginning of its range. For example, adding 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 1
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var ui: UInt8 = 255
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  results in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 0
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You may prefer unsigned integers when negative values are not required, when you are not designing a public API, or when you want to maximize the usable positive range.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

