\fs40 \cf2 Floating-point approximations and comparisons\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Because floating-point values are approximate, they often cannot represent the exact mathematical value they are intended to model.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Rounding errors. Rounding may produce unexpected results. For example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 1/3
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  cannot be represented exactly in floating-point formats. As more floating-point operations are performed, rounding errors may accumulate.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Space between consecutive numbers. The distance between consecutive representable values varies across the range of a floating-point type. Near zero, values are densely packed. For large positive or negative numbers, the spacing can exceed 1, making it impossible to represent some consecutive integers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Because values are approximate, it is rarely useful to compare floating-point numbers using the equality operator (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ==
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ). For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  big_num \cf5 =\cf17  \cf18 1.0e16\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  bigger_num \cf5 =\cf17  big_num \cf5 +\cf17  \cf18 1.0\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 big_num \cf5 ==\cf17  bigger_num\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 True\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Comparison operators (such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 <
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 >=
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) work as expected with floating-point values. To test whether two values are equal within a tolerance, use the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/math/math/isclose/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 math.isclose()}} function to compare whether two floating-point numbers are equal within a specified tolerance.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Mojo-supported floating-point types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In the following table, the eXmX format (for example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float8_e5m2
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float8_e4m3fn
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) refers to the number of bits allocated to a floating-point number's exponent and mantissa.\
All IEEE 754 floating-point formats use an implied leading 1. That means 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is e8m23 but effectively e8m24, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float16
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is e5m10 but effectively e5m11, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 BFloat16
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is e8m7 but effectively e8m8, and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float8_e4m3fn
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is e4m3 but effectively e4m4.\
In addition to eXmX:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
fn signifies finite numbers only. The numbers are valid floating-point values that are not infinite. NaN is supported.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
uz means unsigned zero. Only +0 is supported, not -0.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Although Mojo supports all these types, these types are not supported on all hardware.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 The B in 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb14 BFloat16
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  stands for Brain, from the Google Brain artificial intelligence research group. Google developed it specifically for their Tensor Processing Units (TPUs) to accelerate machine learning workloads. Therefore the B is a project identifier and not a technical format indicator.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

