\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 GPU-only floating-point types are supported on specific accelerator hardware and may not be available on all GPUs.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 AI-optimized floating-point formats\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Several floating-point types are specifically designed for AI and machine learning workloads, trading precision for memory efficiency and computational throughput.\
BFloat16 (Brain Floating Point) uses the same 8 exponent bits as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , preserving its dynamic range, but uses 7 explicit bits (8 effective bits) for the mantissa compared to Float32's 23 bits. This makes it ideal for neural network training where gradient magnitudes vary widely but high precision is less critical.\
8-bit formats (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float8_e5m2
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float8_e4m3fn
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and their 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fnuz
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variants) are ultra-compact formats for AI accelerators where memory bandwidth is the primary bottleneck. The naming indicates bit allocation: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 e5m2
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  means 5 exponent bits and 2 mantissa bits. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fnuz
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  suffix additionally denotes unsigned zero (no -0), used in AMD hardware.\
4-bit format (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float4_e2m1fn
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) offers extreme compression with only 2 exponent bits and 1 mantissa bit, used in specialized inference scenarios where accuracy can be traded for maximum throughput.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Numeric literals\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

