\cf2 At compile time, the literal types are arbitrary-precision (also called infinite-precision) values, so the compiler can perform compile-time calculations without overflow or rounding errors.\
At runtime the values are converted to finite-precision types\'97
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for integer values, and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for floating-point values. (This process of converting a value that can only exist at compile time into a runtime value is called materialization.)\
The following code sample shows the difference between an arbitrary-precision calculation and the same calculation done using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values at runtime, which suffers from rounding errors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  arbitrary_precision \cf5 =\cf17  \cf18 3.0\cf17  \cf5 *\cf17  \cf5 (\cf18 4.0\cf17  \cf5 /\cf17  \cf18 3.0\cf17  \cf5 -\cf17  \cf18 1.0\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb16 # use a variable to force the following calculation to occur at runtime\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  three \cf5 =\cf17  \cf18 3.0\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  finite_precision \cf5 =\cf17  three \cf5 *\cf17  \cf5 (\cf18 4.0\cf17  \cf5 /\cf17  three \cf5 -\cf17  \cf18 1.0\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 arbitrary_precision\cf5 ,\cf17  finite_precision\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

