\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 DType
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To support high-performance numeric processing, Mojo uses the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/simd/SIMD/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 SIMD}} type as the basis for its numeric types. SIMD (single instruction, multiple data) is a processor technology that allows you to perform an operation on an entire set of operands at once. Mojo's 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type abstracts SIMD operations. A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value represents a SIMD vector\'97that is, a fixed-size array of values that can fit into a processor's register. SIMD vectors are defined by two {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 parameters}}:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value, defining the data type in the vector (for example, 32-bit floating-point numbers).\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The number of elements in the vector, which must be a power of two.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For example, you can define a vector of four 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  vec \cf5 =\cf17  SIMD\cf5 [\cf17 DType\cf5 .\cf17 float32\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 4\cf5 ](\cf18 3.0\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 2.0\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 2.0\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 1.0\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Math operations on SIMD values are applied elementwise, on each individual element in the vector. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  vec1 \cf5 =\cf17  SIMD\cf5 [\cf17 DType\cf5 .\cf17 int8\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 4\cf5 ](\cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 3\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 5\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 7\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  vec2 \cf5 =\cf17  SIMD\cf5 [\cf17 DType\cf5 .\cf17 int8\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 4\cf5 ](\cf18 1\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 3\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 4\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  product \cf5 =\cf17  vec1 \cf5 *\cf17  vec2\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 product\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 [2, 6, 15, 28]\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Scalar values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  module defines several {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/metaprogramming/comptime-evaluation/#comptime-values"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 comptime
\fs32 \cb1  values}} that function as type aliases\'97shorthand names for different types of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  vectors. In particular, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Scalar
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type is just a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  vector with a single element. The numeric types listed in \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Table 1\cf2 \ulnone , like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int8
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are actually type aliases for different types of scalar values:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 comptime\cf17  Scalar \cf5 =\cf17  SIMD\cf5 [\cf17 size\cf5 =\cf18 1\cf5 ]\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 comptime\cf17  Int8 \cf5 =\cf17  Scalar\cf5 [\cf17 DType\cf5 .\cf17 int8\cf5 ]\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 comptime\cf17  Float32 \cf5 =\cf17  Scalar\cf5 [\cf17 DType\cf5 .\cf17 float32\cf5 ]\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This may seem a little confusing at first, but it means that whether you're working with a single 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value or a vector of float32 values, the math operations go through exactly the same code path.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 The 
\fs35 \cf10 \cb12 DType
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1  type\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct describes the different data types that a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  vector can hold, and defines a number of utility functions for operating on those data types. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct defines a set of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/#comptime-members"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul comptime
\fs32 \cb1  members}} that act as identifiers for the different data types, like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType.int8
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType.float32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . You use these 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members when declaring a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  vector:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  v\cf5 :\cf17  SIMD\cf5 [\cf17 DType\cf5 .\cf17 float64\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 16\cf5 ]\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Note that 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType.float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  isn't a type, it's a value that describes a data type. You can't create a variable with the type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType.float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . You can create a variable with the type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD[DType.float64, 1]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which is the same thing).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 from\cf17  std\cf5 .\cf17 utils\cf5 .\cf17 numerics \cf5 import\cf17  max_finite\cf5 ,\cf17  min_finite\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb16 def\cf17  describeDType\cf5 [\cf17 dtype\cf5 :\cf17  DType\cf5 ]()\cf17  raises\cf5 :\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16     \cf5 print(\cf17 dtype\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf21 "is floating-point:"\cf5 ,\cf17  dtype\cf5 .\cf17 is_floating_point\cf5 ())\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16     \cf5 print(\cf17 dtype\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf21 "is integral:"\cf5 ,\cf17  dtype\cf5 .\cf17 is_integral\cf5 ())\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16     \cf5 print(\cf21 "Min/max finite values for"\cf5 ,\cf17  dtype\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16     \cf5 print(\cf17 min_finite\cf5 [\cf17 dtype\cf5 ](),\cf17  max_finite\cf5 [\cf17 dtype\cf5 ]())\cf17 \cb1 \
\
\cb16 describeDType\cf5 [\cf17 DType\cf5 .\cf17 float32\cf5 ]()\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 float32 is floating-point: True\cb1 \
\cb16 float32 is integral: False\cb1 \
\cb16 Min/max finite values for float32\cb1 \
\cb16 -3.4028234663852886e+38 3.4028234663852886e+38\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 There are several other data types in the standard library that also use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  abstraction.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Numeric type conversion\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#constructors-and-implicit-conversion"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs32 \cf5 \ul Constructors and implicit conversion}}
\fs32  documents the circumstances in which Mojo automatically converts a value from one type to another. Importantly, numeric {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/operators/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul operators}} don't automatically narrow or widen operands to a common type.\
You can explicitly convert a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value to a different 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type either by invoking its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/simd/SIMD/#cast"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul cast()}} method or by passing it as an argument to the constructor of the target type. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 simd1 \cf5 =\cf17  SIMD\cf5 [\cf17 DType\cf5 .\cf17 float32\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 4\cf5 ](\cf18 2.2\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 3.3\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 4.4\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 5.5\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16 simd2 \cf5 =\cf17  SIMD\cf5 [\cf17 DType\cf5 .\cf17 int16\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 4\cf5 ](-\cf18 1\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf5 -\cf18 3\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 4\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16 simd3 \cf5 =\cf17  simd1 \cf5 *\cf17  simd2\cf5 .\cf17 cast\cf5 [\cf17 DType\cf5 .\cf17 float32\cf5 ]()\cf17   \cf6 # Convert with cast() method\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf21 "simd3:"\cf5 ,\cf17  simd3\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16 simd4 \cf5 =\cf17  simd2 \cf5 +\cf17  SIMD\cf5 [\cf17 DType\cf5 .\cf17 int16\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 4\cf5 ](\cf17 simd1\cf5 )\cf17   \cf6 # Convert with SIMD constructor\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf21 "simd4:"\cf5 ,\cf17  simd4\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 simd3: [-2.2, 6.6, -13.200001, 22.0]\cb1 \
\cb16 simd4: [1, 5, 1, 9]\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can convert a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Scalar
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value by passing it as an argument to the constructor of the target type. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  my_int\cf5 :\cf17  Int16 \cf5 =\cf17  \cf18 12\cf17                  \cf6 # SIMD[DType.int16, 1]\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  my_float\cf5 :\cf17  Float32 \cf5 =\cf17  \cf18 0.75\cf17            \cf6 # SIMD[DType.float32, 1]\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16 result \cf5 =\cf17  Float32\cf5 (\cf17 my_int\cf5 )\cf17  \cf5 *\cf17  my_float    \cf6 # Result is SIMD[DType.float32, 1]\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf21 "Result:"\cf5 ,\cf17  result\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 Result: 9.0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can convert a scalar value of any numeric type to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  by passing the value to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/int/Int/#__init__"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Int()}} constructor method. Additionally, you can pass an instance of any struct that implements the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/int/Intable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Intable}} trait or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/int/IntableRaising/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul IntableRaising}} trait to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor to convert that instance to an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
You can convert an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IntLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type by passing the value to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/uint/UInt/#__init__"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul UInt()}} constructor. You can't convert other numeric types to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  directly, though you can first convert them to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and then to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Strings\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Strings are Mojo's primary text type. They store UTF-8 encoded text and provide a safe, ergonomic interface for string manipulation.\
Mojo's 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type is a mutable string. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  supports a variety of operators and common methods:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  s\cf5 :\cf17  String \cf5 =\cf17  \cf21 "Testing"\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16 s \cf5 +=\cf17  \cf21 " Mojo strings"\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 s\cf5 )\cf17  \cf6 # Testing Mojo strings\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Construction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Many standard library types conform to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/str/Stringable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Stringable}} trait, which indicates that a value can be converted into a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String(...)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor.\
The built-in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/io/io/print/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul print()}} function accepts values that conform to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait. For values that are only 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Stringable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String(value)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  first and then pass the resulting 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 print()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String(value)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to explicitly convert a value to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  s \cf5 =\cf17  \cf21 "Items in list: "\cf17  \cf5 +\cf17  String\cf5 (\cf18 5\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 s\cf5 )\cf17  \cf6 # Items in list: 5\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Or, use the string constructor with variadic 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Stringable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  types, so you don't have to call 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  on each value:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  s \cf5 =\cf17  String\cf5 (\cf21 "Items in list: "\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 5\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 s\cf5 )\cf17  \cf6 # Items in list: 5\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 String formatting\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 format()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method inserts values into a string using manual or automatic positional indexing. Replacement fields use braces:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 print(\cf21 "\{0\} \{1\} \{0\}"\cf5 .\cf22 format\cf5 (\cf21 "Mojo"\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 1.125\cf5 ))\cf17   \cf6 # Mojo 1.125 Mojo\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf21 "\{\} \{\}"\cf5 .\cf22 format\cf5 (\cf18 True\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf21 "hello world"\cf5 ))\cf17   \cf6 # True hello world\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo's 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 TString
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (template string) replaces the functionality of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 format()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method with direct expressions and better performance characteristics.\

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 TString
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 s work like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 format()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but they insert {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/format/Writable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Writable}} representations of expressions into replacement fields. This provides safe and flexible string processing:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  count \cf5 =\cf17  \cf18 3\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  items \cf5 =\cf17  \cf21 "apples"\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  template \cf5 =\cf17  t\cf21 "Give me \{count\} \{items\}."\cf17  \cf6 # Template string\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 template\cf5 )\cf17  \cf6 # Output: Give me 3 apples.\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 TString
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values are lazy. They don't allocate until you explicitly construct a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  x \cf5 =\cf17  \cf18 41\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 t\cf21 "The answer is \{x + 1\}"\cf5 )\cf17   \cf6 # The answer is 42 (no allocation)\cf17 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  name \cf5 =\cf17  \cf21 "Nate"\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  template \cf5 =\cf17  t\cf21 "Hello, \{name\}!"\cf17  \cf6 # template creation\cf17 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 template\cf5 )\cf17           \cf6 # Hello, Nate! (no allocation)\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  s \cf5 =\cf17  String\cf5 (\cf17 template\cf5 )\cf17  \cf6 # explicitly construct a string\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 TString
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 s can add arbitrary expressions within the replacement fields:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  \cf22 list\cf17  \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 [\cf18 1\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 3\cf5 ]\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 t\cf21 "\{list[0] + list[1]\}"\cf5 )\cf17  \cf6 # 3\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Raw strings\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In Mojo, raw strings are string literals prefixed with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 r
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . If you ran the following command it would print on one line, not two, because raw strings prevent backslash escape sequences from being interpreted:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 print(\cf21 r"Hello\\nWorld"\cf5 )\cf17   \cf6 # Hello\\nWorld, with the backslash and n\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Raw strings help with regular expressions, code generation, serialization code, and other applications where you want to use escape sequences as literal entries in your string. Unlike normal strings, escapes aren't processed.\
Rawness applies to all forms of strings: single line, multi-line, docstrings, and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 TString
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 s. Raw 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 TString
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 s support interpolation but won't expand escape sequences:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  name \cf5 =\cf17  \cf21 "Nate"\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 rt\cf21 "Hello,\\t\{name\}."\cf5 )\cf17   \cf6 # Hello,\\tNate., with the backslash and t\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Raw strings still need a way to terminate, so if you have to use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 "
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  within your content, use an alternate form of quote, such as single quote (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 '
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) or triple quotes, (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 """
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 '''
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) to enclose it:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf21 \cb16 r'She said, "Hello, World!"'\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 String literals\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 As with numeric types, the standard library includes a string literal type used to represent literal strings in the program source. String literals are enclosed in either single or double quotes.\
Adjacent literals are concatenated together, so you can define a long string using a series of literals broken up over several lines:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 comptime s = "A very long string which is "\cb1 \
\cb16         "broken into two literals for legibility."\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 To define a multi-line string, enclose the literal in three single or double quotes:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 comptime s = """\cb1 \
\cb16 Multi-line string literals let you\cb1 \
\cb16 enter long blocks of text, including\cb1 \
\cb16 newlines."""\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Note that the triple double quote form is also used for API documentation strings.\
A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  will materialize to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when used at run-time:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 comptime\cf17  param \cf5 =\cf17  \cf21 "foo"\cf17         \cf6 # type = StringLiteral\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  runtime_value \cf5 =\cf17  \cf21 "bar"\cf17   \cf6 # type = String\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  runtime_value2 \cf5 =\cf17  param \cf6 # type = String\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Booleans\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo's 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Bool
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type represents a boolean value. It can take one of two values, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 True
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 False
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . You can negate a boolean value using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 not
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operator.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  conditionA \cf5 =\cf17  \cf18 False\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  conditionB\cf5 :\cf17  Bool\cb1 \
\cb16 conditionB \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 not\cf17  conditionA\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 conditionA\cf5 ,\cf17  conditionB\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 False True\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Many types have a boolean representation. Any type that implements the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/bool/Boolable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Boolable}} trait has a boolean representation. As a general principle, collections evaluate as True if they contain any elements, False if they are empty; strings evaluate as True if they have a non-zero length.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Tuples\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo's 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Tuple
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type represents an immutable tuple consisting of zero or more values, separated by commas. Tuples can consist of multiple types and you can index into tuples in multiple ways.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb16 # Tuples are immutable and can hold multiple types\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \cb16 example_tuple \cf5 =\cf17  Tuple\cf5 [\cf17 Int\cf5 ,\cf17  String\cf5 ](\cf18 1\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf21 "Example"\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb16 # Assign multiple variables at once\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \cb16 x\cf5 ,\cf17  y \cf5 =\cf17  example_tuple\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 x\cf5 ,\cf17  y\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb16 # Get individual values with an index\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \cb16 s \cf5 =\cf17  example_tuple\cf5 [\cf18 1\cf5 ]\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 s\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 1 Example\cb1 \
\cb16 Example\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can also create a tuple without explicit typing.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 example_tuple \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 (\cf18 1\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf21 "Example"\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16 s \cf5 =\cf17  example_tuple\cf5 [\cf18 1\cf5 ]\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 s\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 Example\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Collection types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The Mojo standard library also includes a set of basic collection types that can be used to build more complex data structures:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/list/List/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul List}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
, a dynamically-sized array of items.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/dict/Dict/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Dict}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
, an associative array of key-value pairs.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/set/Set/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Set}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
, an unordered collection of unique items.\
\ls11\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/optional/Optional/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul Optional}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
 represents a value that may or may not be present.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The collection types are generic types: while a given collection can only hold a specific type of value (such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ), you specify the type at compile time using a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul parameter}}. For example, you can create a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  l\cf5 :\cf17  List\cf5 [\cf17 Int\cf5 ]\cf17  \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 [\cf18 1\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 3\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 4\cf5 ]\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb16 # l.append(3.14) # error: FloatLiteral cannot be converted to Int\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You don't always need to specify the type explicitly. If Mojo can infer the type, you can omit it. For example, when you construct a list from a set of integer literals, Mojo creates a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List[Int]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb16 # Inferred type == List[Int]\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  l1 \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 [\cf18 1\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 3\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 4\cf5 ]\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Where you need a more flexible collection, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/utils/variant/Variant/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Variant}} type can hold different types of values. For example, a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Variant[Int32, Float64]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can hold either an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value at any given time. (Using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Variant
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is not covered in this section, see the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/utils/variant/Variant/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul API docs}} for more information.)\
The following sections give brief introduction to the main collection types.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 List\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/list/List/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul List}}
\fs32  is a dynamically-sized array of elements. List elements need to conform to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/Copyable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Copyable}} trait. Most of the common standard library primitives, like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 SIMD
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  conform to this trait. You can create a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  by passing the element type as a parameter, like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  l \cf5 =\cf17  List\cf5 [\cf17 String\cf5 ]()\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type supports a subset of the Python 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 list
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  API, including the ability to append to the list, pop items out of the list, and access list items using subscript notation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  \cf22 list\cf17  \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 [\cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 3\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 5\cf5 ]\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf22 \cb16 list\cf5 .\cf17 append\cf5 (\cf18 7\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf22 \cb16 list\cf5 .\cf17 append\cf5 (\cf18 11\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf21 "Popping last item from list: "\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf22 list\cf5 .\cf17 pop\cf5 ())\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 for\cf17  idx \cf5 in\cf17  \cf22 range\cf5 (\cf22 len\cf5 (\cf22 list\cf5 )):\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16       \cf5 print(\cf22 list\cf5 [\cf17 idx\cf5 ],\cf17  end\cf5 =\cf21 ", "\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 Popping last item from list:  11\cb1 \
\cb16 2, 3, 5, 7,\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Note that the previous code sample leaves out the type parameter when creating the list. Because the list is being created with a set of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values, Mojo can infer the type from the arguments.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo supports list, set, and dictionary literals for collection initialization:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf6 \cb16 # List literal, element type infers to Int.\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf5 \cb16 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
var\cf17  nums \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 [\cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 3\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 5\cf5 ]\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf17 \cb16 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf19 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf20 \uc0\u8232 \cf19 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cb16 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \cb1 You can also use an explicit type if you want a specific element type:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  \cf22 list\cf17  \cf5 :\cf17  List\cf5 [\cf17 UInt8\cf5 ]\cf17  \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 [\cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 3\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 5\cf5 ]\cf17 \cb1 \
\ls12\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf17 \cb16 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf19 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf20 \uc0\u8232 \cf19 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cb16 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \cb1 You can also use list "comprehensions" for compact conditional initialization:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  list2 \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 [\cf17 x\cf5 *\cf17 Int\cf5 (\cf17 y\cf5 )\cf17  \cf5 for\cf17  x \cf5 in\cf17  nums \cf5 for\cf17  y \cf5 in\cf17  \cf22 list\cf17  \cf5 if\cf17  x \cf5 !=\cf17  \cf18 3\cf5 ]\cf17 \cb1 \
\ls12\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf17 \cb16 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf19 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf20 \uc0\u8232 \cf19 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
You can't 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 print()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  a list, or convert it directly into a string.\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf6 \cb16 # Does not work\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf5 \cb16 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
print(\cf22 list\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf17 \cb16 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf19 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf20 \uc0\u8232 \cf19 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cb16 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \cb1 As shown above, you can print the individual elements in a list as long as they're a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/str/Stringable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Stringable}} type.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Iterating a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  returns an immutable {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/lifetimes/#working-with-references"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul reference}} to each item:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  \cf22 list\cf17  \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 [\cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 3\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 4\cf5 ]\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 for\cf17  item \cf5 in\cf17  \cf22 list\cf5 :\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16       \cf5 print(\cf17 item\cf5 ,\cf17  end\cf5 =\cf21 ", "\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 2, 3, 4,\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If you would like to mutate the elements of the list, capture the reference to the element with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instead of making a copy:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  \cf22 list\cf17  \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 [\cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 3\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf18 4\cf5 ]\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 for\cf17  \cf5 ref\cf17  item \cf5 in\cf17  \cf22 list\cf5 :\cf17      \cf6 # Capture a ref to the list element\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16       \cf5 print(\cf17 item\cf5 ,\cf17  end\cf5 =\cf21 ", "\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16       item \cf5 =\cf17  \cf18 0\cf17   \cf6 # Mutates the element inside the list\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf21 "\\nAfter loop:"\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf22 list\cf5 [\cf18 0\cf5 ],\cf17  \cf22 list\cf5 [\cf18 1\cf5 ],\cf17  \cf22 list\cf5 [\cf18 2\cf5 ])\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 2, 3, 4,\cb1 \
\cb16 After loop: 0 0 0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can see that the original loop entries were modified.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Dict\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/dict/Dict/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Dict}} type is an associative array that holds key-value pairs. You can create a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Dict
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  by specifying the key type and value type as parameters and using dictionary literals:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb16 # Empty dictionary\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  empty_dict\cf5 :\cf17  Dict\cf5 [\cf17 String\cf5 ,\cf17  Float64\cf5 ]\cf17  \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 \{\}\cf17 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb16 # Dictionary with initial key-value pairs\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  values\cf5 :\cf17  Dict\cf5 [\cf17 String\cf5 ,\cf17  Float64\cf5 ]\cf17  \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 \{\cf21 "pi"\cf5 :\cf17  \cf18 3.14159\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf21 "e"\cf5 :\cf17  \cf18 2.71828\cf5 \}\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can also use the constructor syntax:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  values \cf5 =\cf17  Dict\cf5 [\cf17 String\cf5 ,\cf17  Float64\cf5 ]()\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The dictionary's key type must conform to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/dict/#keyelement"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul KeyElement}} trait, and value elements must conform to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/Copyable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Copyable}} trait.\
You can insert and remove key-value pairs, update the value assigned to a key, and iterate through keys, values, or items in the dictionary.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Dict
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  iterators all yield {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/lifetimes/#working-with-references"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul references}}, which are copied into the declared name by default, but you can use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  marker to avoid the copy:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  d\cf5 :\cf17  Dict\cf5 [\cf17 String\cf5 ,\cf17  Float64\cf5 ]\cf17  \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 \{\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16     \cf21 "plasticity"\cf5 :\cf17  \cf18 3.1\cf5 ,\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16     \cf21 "elasticity"\cf5 :\cf17  \cf18 1.3\cf5 ,\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16     \cf21 "electricity"\cf5 :\cf17  \cf18 9.7\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 \}\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 for\cf17  item \cf5 in\cf17  d\cf5 .\cf17 items\cf5 ():\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16     \cf5 print(\cf17 item\cf5 .\cf17 key\cf5 ,\cf17  item\cf5 .\cf17 value\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 plasticity 3.1000000000000001\cb1 \
\cb16 elasticity 1.3\cb1 \
\cb16 electricity 9.6999999999999993\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 This is an unmeasurable micro-optimization in this case, but is useful when working with types that aren't 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Set\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/set/Set/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Set}} type represents a set of unique values. You can add and remove elements from the set, test whether a value exists in the set, and perform set algebra operations, like unions and intersections between two sets.\
Sets are generic and the element type must conform to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/dict/#keyelement"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul KeyElement}} trait. Like lists and dictionaries, sets support standard literal syntax, as well as generator comprehensions:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 i_like \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 \{\cf21 "sushi"\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf21 "ice cream"\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf21 "tacos"\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf21 "pho"\cf5 \}\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16 you_like \cf5 =\cf17  \cf5 \{\cf21 "burgers"\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf21 "tacos"\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf21 "salad"\cf5 ,\cf17  \cf21 "ice cream"\cf5 \}\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16 we_like \cf5 =\cf17  i_like\cf5 .\cf17 intersection\cf5 (\cf17 you_like\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf21 "We both like:"\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 for\cf17  item \cf5 in\cf17  we_like\cf5 :\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16     \cf5 print(\cf21 "-"\cf5 ,\cf17  item\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 We both like:\cb1 \
\cb16 - ice cream\cb1 \
\cb16 - tacos\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Optional\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 An {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/optional/Optional/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Optional}} represents a value that may or may not be present. Like the other collection types, it is generic, and can hold any type that conforms to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/Copyable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Copyable}} trait.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb16 # Two ways to initialize an Optional with a value\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  opt1 \cf5 =\cf17  Optional\cf5 (\cf18 5\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  opt2\cf5 :\cf17  Optional\cf5 [\cf17 Int\cf5 ]\cf17  \cf5 =\cf17  \cf18 5\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb16 # Two ways to initialize an Optional with no value\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  opt3 \cf5 =\cf17  Optional\cf5 [\cf17 Int\cf5 ]()\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  opt4\cf5 :\cf17  Optional\cf5 [\cf17 Int\cf5 ]\cf17  \cf5 =\cf17  \cf18 None\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 An 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Optional
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  evaluates as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 True
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when it holds a value, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 False
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  otherwise. If the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Optional
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  holds a value, you can retrieve a reference to the value using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 value()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method. But calling 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 value()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  on an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Optional
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with no value results in undefined behavior, so you should always guard a call to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 value()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  inside a conditional that checks whether a value exists.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  opt\cf5 :\cf17  Optional\cf5 [\cf17 String\cf5 ]\cf17  \cf5 =\cf17  \cf21 "Testing"\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 if\cf17  opt\cf5 :\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16     \cf5 var\cf17  value_ref \cf5 =\cf17  opt\cf5 .\cf17 value\cf5 ()\cf17 \cb1 \
\cb16     \cf5 print(\cf17 value_ref\cf5 )\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb16 Testing\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Alternately, you can use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 or_else()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method, which returns the stored value if there is one, or a user-specified default value otherwise:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 var\cf17  custom_greeting\cf5 :\cf17  Optional\cf5 [\cf17 String\cf5 ]\cf17  \cf5 =\cf17  \cf18 None\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 custom_greeting\cf5 .\cf17 or_else\cf5 (\cf21 "Hello"\cf5 ))\cf17 \cb1 \
\
\cb16 custom_greeting \cf5 =\cf17  \cf21 "Hi"\cf17 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb16 print(\cf17 custom_greeting\cf5 .\cf17 or_else\cf5 (\cf21 "Hello"\cf5 ))\cf17 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

